course: dictionary
lesson: 12180
title: Happening at the same time
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Concurrent means happening or existing at the same time. Look for clues in the words around it. «The school fair and the football match were concurrent, so Sam could not go to both.» Sam could not be at two places at once, so concurrent must mean at the same time.
explain: Maths gives the word a second meaning. «Three lines drawn on the paper were concurrent, because they all crossed at one point.» The lines all meet in one place, so concurrent can mean meeting at the same point. Ask yourself what would fit: at the same time, or in the same place?
explain: Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence. Try «The school fair and the football match were happening at the same time, so Sam could not go to both.» It still makes sense, so your meaning is right. The adverb is concurrently, as in «The two bands played concurrently.»
fact: Concurrent means happening or existing at the same time.
fact: In maths, lines are concurrent if they all cross at one point.
fact: In a court sentence, concurrent means two sentences are served at the same time, not one after the other.
fact: «Concurrent» is an adjective, and «concurrently» is the adverb that tells you how something happens.
fact: In computing, concurrent programs run at the same time.
exercise: «The school play and the sports day were concurrent, so Mia had to choose one.» What does concurrent mean? | Happening one after the other | *Happening at the same time | Happening in the rain | Happening very quietly
exercise: «Three lines were concurrent, because they all crossed at one point.» Find and copy the word that tells you where the lines met. | three | *point | lines | paper
exercise: Sam says concurrent means far apart. Why is Sam wrong? | *Concurrent means at the same time or in the same place, not far apart | Far apart is the opposite idea, because concurrent means at the same time or in the same place | Concurrent means the same as a clock | Concurrent means very loud
test: «The two clocks chimed concurrently.» What does concurrently mean here? | Slowly, one at a time | Backwards | *At the same time | Very quietly
test: Lena asks, «Can I join two concurrent clubs that meet on the same afternoon?» What is the answer? | *No, because concurrent clubs happen at the same time, so you must choose one | Yes, because they are different clubs | Yes, if you go very fast between them
exam: Which sentence uses concurrent correctly? | The two trains left the station concurrently, at the same time, on the same platform | *Three concurrent lines all meet at one point | My concurrent dinner was very tasty | Concurrent is the name of a cat
